A||N||7||LUV ALL FIEROS||overheating problems||||8||
Z||000000||LUV ALL FIEROS||07-13-2001||02:13 AM||||help! i have an 86 gt, with a 2.8 , with 600 miles on it now. problem, it has been overheating since i got the new engine in it. it runs at around 1/4 to 1/3 on the temp gauge without the thermostat., i have used the special coolant fill procedure, and it seems to run cool, till i get on the throttle a good bit. then it goes up to the red, and beyond, and i shut it down, pull the thermostat, and drive off, well, today, while filling and trying to remove the air, i kept adding, and found out that it was going into the overflow bottle with the radiator cap on! so i bought a new cap, installed the thermo, drove 38 miles to work, and it appeared to have fixed it, but on the way home, it got hot again, and while i was feeling the hose, by the thermostat, i reved it up, and the hose would collapse and expand when the idle returned, rev up, collapse, so i removed the thermostat again, to limp home, thoroughly disgusted again. finally, the question, i appologize for the lengthy article, but i find that you can't provide too much info when describing a problem, anyway, why would the hose collapse on the side that supposedly sends the coolant to the engine? i mean, i could see it on the return line, but on the line coming from the engine? could the hose collapse from alot of air in it? will a new hose help it? please help, i am really getting sick of this problem. thanks for all the help!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||07-13-2001||07:12 AM||||If the hose is collapsing it would seem that something is restricting coolant flow into the radiator. Check to see if the coolant tubes running under the car have been crushed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||baptistheart||07-13-2001||07:50 AM||||I might have missed something here but It sounds like it's your thermostat to me I had a Trans am that did that to me once. But if i'm wrong i'm sure you'll find the answer here somewhere. and Welcome to the forum! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||07-13-2001||08:19 AM||||Your hose is collapsing because the there is air in the cooling system. <BR>top off the front first the the rear. run the car for a few minutes to warm up the engine but not HOT! crack the front cap until the coolant comes out BE CAREFULL the coolant may be hot.<BR>Open the rear cap slowly and top off the system. fill the coolant bottle and take it for a spin. Let the engine cool fully and top off the rear again.<BR>Every so often pull the rear cap and check the level and top it until it stays full.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||07-13-2001||03:29 PM||||Make sure your new rad. cap in non-vented too...Or so everyone says. I needed a new one, they gave me a vented one, and iv used it for over 3 months now with no problems at all...might be somthing to check also.||||reg||1
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||07-13-2001||05:37 PM||||Ive  never seen a hose do what you describe.Ive heard that they do expand and contract like you say.I dont have any solution for you to correct this problem yhat you are haveing.but if this ever happened to me I would get me a radiator presure tester and connect it to the radiator and start the motor.I would and watch the presure gauge.I would want to make sure that I didnt have a blown head gasket or a cracked  cyl.head.and also check to see that the coolant tubes that run under the car are not damaged in any way shape or form. Ive heard other people desribe cooling system problems of all sorts anfind that they had a dent in one these tubesthey repaired the dent  and wala the problem is solved good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||07-14-2001||11:00 AM||||Hey Luv!<BR>Your problem is obvious to me!<BR>The hose should never colapse...<BR>Is it an old hose?<BR>Replace it and check the others...<BR>Mine did the same, the hose was soooo soft...<BR>I also replaced the other hoses and heater hoses while I was getting dirty...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000007||LUV ALL FIEROS||07-14-2001||06:00 PM||||THANKS TO ALL WHO REPLIED. I BELIEVE I HAVE IT SOLVED, ANYWAY, IT OVERHEATED THIS MORNING ON THE WAY TO WORK, SO I PUT A FLOOR LIFT LEG UNDER THE PASSENGER REAR SIDE, AND JACKED UP THE CORNER TILL THE FRONT WHEEL STARTED TO COME UP, AND FILLED, AND RAN, OPENED THE CAP, AND THERE WAS LIKE A FOAM, WHICH I HOPE WAS AIR mixing with the water. and i did this several times, then installed a lower 180 degree thermostat from advance, and started, it climed high, but not in the red, and i drove slowly and cautiously till i felt good enough to take it out on I270, at speeds of up to 70 mph for 20 minutes, and the gauge stayed on 1/4 to 1/3 all the way home. hopefully, this will be the last time. thanks again! you were all very helpful, and hope that i can return the favor sometime to another fellow fiero owner in need.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
